noi_dung:
{align:justify|There was a laywoman who had received the Bodhisattva precepts, a Mrs. X, vegetarian for over thirty years. The whole latter half of her life was spent eating vegetarian and worshipping the Buddha. After she passed away, her family came to the temple to invite Master Nhan Ba Thiet to chant sutras and dedicate the merit to her. Naturally he gladly accepted.}
{align:justify|That night Master Nhan Ba Thiet suddenly beheld a most peculiar vision: a sow giving birth to a dozen-odd piglets, all white-furred — except for a single one with a black ring across its back. He saw it but paid it little mind.}
{align:justify|The next day Mrs. X's family came again to see Nhan Ba Thiet and asked:}
{align:justify|"Our mother ate vegetarian for many years, received the Bodhisattva precepts, and had a very good heart — so after her death, where will she be reborn? Might she have gone to the Land of Ultimate Bliss of Amitabha Buddha?"}
{align:justify|Master Nhan Ba Thiet replied:}
{align:justify|"Let me watch tonight's dream and see."}
{align:justify|That night Nhan Ba Thiet saw exactly what he had seen before: "that pig" in a brick-built pen in nearby Ngu On village — most clearly.}
{align:justify|On the third day Mrs. X's son came to inquire, and Nhan Ba Thiet, unwilling to speak plainly, only asked:}
{align:justify|"Before your mother passed, were there any signs?"}
{align:justify|Her son answered:}
{align:justify|"No, Venerable Sir. Near the end she lay unconscious, with no final words."}
{align:justify|Nhan Ba Thiet said:}
{align:justify|"Then it does not bode well."}
{align:justify|And the Master thought to himself: "Diligent Buddhist disciples usually have some foreknowledge, some lucid understanding. A lifelong vegetarian like Mrs. X, good-natured, fond of helping others, and devout in offerings to the temple — how could she at death be reborn as an animal? How could that be?"}
{align:justify|Master Nhan Ba Thiet said nothing to Mrs. X's son, only instructing:}
{align:justify|"Go home and investigate — ask the whole family whether your mother said anything before she died. For before passing she still had lucid moments; she must have spoken of something, mentioned something — such as a wish she longed to fulfill but never could, and so clung to it in her heart. Go home and ask."}
{align:justify|The son obeyed and investigated at home. He learned that on the evening three days before her death, his wife had nursed the old lady and heard her say over and over:}
{align:justify|"Slaughter that pig — quickly!"}
{align:justify|She repeated it again and again. Then she died.}
{align:justify|Hearing the family's account, Master Nhan Ba Thiet said:}
{align:justify|"Now it is clear. I can confirm: her fall into this realm today comes entirely from clinging to a deviant thought."}
{align:justify|Then Master Nhan Ba Thiet told Mrs. X's kin:}
{align:justify|"Quite far from your home lies a village called Ngu On, where a family raises pigs. There a sow has just farrowed a litter of a dozen-odd piglets. Go there and you will see one piglet most special and unlike the rest. Buy it quickly and bring it back — I will give it refuge."}
{align:justify|He did not yet dare tell them outright that it was their mother's new body.}
{align:justify|They searched for three days until they reached Ngu On and found the piglet with exactly the coloring Master Nhan Ba Thiet had described. Stranger still, the moment the piglet saw them it ran over, delighted and friendly. Seeing the pig's manner, Mrs. X's kin needed no words — each understood in silence and guessed this must be their mother. Unspoken, they grieved. They quickly bought the pig, brought it to the temple, and asked Nhan Ba Thiet to give it refuge.}
{align:justify|Refuge given, her children discussed taking it home to raise. Nhan Ba Thiet said:}
{align:justify|"No. Refuge taken, it must be carried deep into the mountains and released."}
{align:justify|The family worried that once released, fierce beasts would devour it.}
{align:justify|Nhan Ba Thiet said:}
{align:justify|"Would you rather have her stay in the animal realm forever?"}
{align:justify|The family took it to release, and before long it died. The pig dead, she was reborn into the human realm.}
{align:justify|Nhan Ba Thiet said: "Let this be a lesson to warn you all: though a lifetime of reciting the Buddha's name and eating vegetarian, if one keeps uttering deviant views and clinging to an unrighteous mind without release, it is supremely dangerous at the hour of death — and one must repay one's own evil thought. A deviant thought can strike its owner with rebounding retribution: however one plots to harm people or creatures, one is harmed first oneself. Why did your mother, before dying, keep demanding the pig be slaughtered? Because in her heart she nursed one thing constantly: hoping that when her eldest grandson married, she would follow Taiwanese custom — slaughtering pigs and goats and holding a lavish wedding feast. This was the heart's wish she forever cherished, carried, and longed to fulfill for her grandson's wedding."}
{align:justify|Since the wish went unfulfilled — the grandson unwed while the grandmother died — the deviant intent lingered in her heart. So at the dying breath, whichever thought in the mind is strongest leads one to rebirth in the realm matching that mind. Near death her heart was all blazing killing-thoughts; she had no longing to recite Amitabha Buddha's name and beg His reception — her mind was all bloodlust, craving to slaughter the pig. Nursed to ripeness, this deviant thought drowned a lifetime of merit and wholesome thoughts, and she had to be reborn as a pig and repay her own bloodthirsty intent.}
{align:justify|Then Master Nhan Ba Thiet concluded:}
{align:justify|"A lifetime of reciting the Buddha and eating vegetarian — fallen, for not releasing deviant views. Deathbed karma is most terrifying. Be careful, be warned!"}
